`vltg_quarterly`). I did not look at industry data. That is n3's judgment.\n\n**Supportive evidence**\n- H1 2026 orders rose 11% year on year, led by utility and data-centre customers.\n- Backlog is C$5.1B. Management says it covers the C$4.2B 2027 guidance, which is about 121% coverage.\n- Customer cancellations are below 1%, so the backlog looks firm.\n- Halbrook, the largest plant, restarted in August at full capacity.\n\n**Cautions**\n- Halbrook was offline for nine weeks in Q2 after a fire in the coil-winding hall. Q2 revenue fell to C$890M, from C$980M in Q1.\n- Management expects to recover the lost shipments only by mid-2027. The delayed shipments should add to 2027 revenue, but they also make it depend on running above normal output.\n- The last four quarters total about C$3.7B (905 + 960 + 980 + 890). Reaching C$4.2B needs roughly 13% growth.\n- That works out to about C$1.05B per quarter, against a Q1 2026 peak of C$980M.\n- Backlog is not revenue. The C$5.1B figure may extend beyond 2027. The excess over guidance is about C$0.9B, or about 21% slack.\n\n**Implication**\n- C$4.2B is achievable if the backlog converts on schedule and the capacity catch-up works. The excess backlog gives modest cushion against slippage.\n- My rough estimate is a range of about C$3.9B to C$4.3B, with the guidance level near the upper-middle of that range. I did not model this precisely.\n- Outside n3's scope, the Q2 market-share loss from 31% to 27% (in `cea-market-share`) bears on whether new orders keep coming.","ttft_ms":2026,"type":"result","duration_ms":24213,"uuid":"8c865d9f-0abe-4a1b-9548-2153fd9c01a5","ttft_stream_ms":1206,"time_to_request_ms":343,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":14,"system_prompt":21,"resume_other":12,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":94,"input_hooks":19,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":28,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":40,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":9,"loop_entry":11,"message_prep":15,"model_config_wait":0,"aut